{"cuisine/culture-of-grenada-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/culture-of-grenada-cuisine","n":"Grenadian cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Grenada","intro":[["Grenada's French colonists brought their culture, as did the African slaves they brought across the Atlantic for agricultural work. Indians have also influenced the island culture in more recent years."],["With the passing of the Slave Trade Act 1807 by the British Parliament and the subsequent abolishing of slavery, indentured labor from India was procured at a very large scale."],["The first ship, named the Maidstone, departed from Calcutta, India on January 27, 1857, and arrived a few months later on May 1. In all, 3,206 East Indians arrived in Grenada by 1885. Only 380 of them returned to India. The Indians made many contributions to Grenada. Indian Arrival Day was celebrated in 2007 on the 150th anniversary, for the first time since the centenary celebration in 1957."],["The Indians later on assimilated with the existing Africans, Europeans and other ethnicities intermarrying with each other. This very much influenced the culture and cuisine of Grenada."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"culture-of-grenada-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Grenada#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Caribbean Pot: Grenada","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"caribbeanpot.com","note":"Grenadian dishes on Chris De La Rosa's large Caribbean recipe site.","url":"https://caribbeanpot.com/tag/grenada-cooking/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"Spice Island Cooking","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"spiceislandcooking.com","note":"Blog of Grenadian home recipes.","url":"https://spiceislandcooking.com/?p=618","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/japanese-regional-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/japanese-regional-cuisine","n":"Japanese regional cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Japan","intro":[["Japanese cuisine has a vast array of regional specialities known as kyōdo ryōri in Japanese, many of them originating from dishes prepared using local ingredients and traditional recipes."],["While \"local\" ingredients are now available nationwide, and some originally regional dishes such as okonomiyaki and Edo-style sushi have spread throughout Japan and are no longer considered as such, many regional specialities survive to this day, with some new ones still being created."],["Regionality is also apparent in many dishes which are served throughout Japan such as ozōni soup. For example, the dashi-based broth for serving udon noodles is heavy on dark soy sauce, similar to soba broth, in eastern Japan, while in western Japan the broth relies more on the complex dashi-flavouring, with a hint of light soy sauce."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"japanese-regional-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_regional_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Kyodo Ryori: Japanese Regional Cuisine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"ny.us.emb-japan.go.jp","note":"Embassy of Japan in New York pages on local dishes by prefecture, with videos and PDF recipes.","url":"https://www.ny.us.emb-japan.go.jp/Foods/kyodoryori/index.html","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Hokkaido: Recipes from the Seas, Fields and Farmlands of Northern Japan","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Tim Anderson, 2024","note":"Hardie Grant book on the regional cooking of Japan's northern island by a US-born chef in London.","url":"https://www.penguin.co.uk/books/466881/hokkaido-by-tim-anderson/9781784885984","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781784885984"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Japanese regional cooking","count":1,"links":[["Nagoya cuisine","cuisine/nagoya-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/4d/Salmon-Ruibe.jpg/500px-Salmon-Ruibe.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"MaedaAkihiko","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salmon-Ruibe.jpg"}},"cuisine/czech-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/czech-cuisine","n":"Czech cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Czechia","intro":[["Czech cuisine includes a variety of staples, dishes, beverages, and snacks which are typical of the broader Central European region. Many dishes are heavily influenced by historical shifts in the availability of various ingredients, particularly during the communist era of Czechoslovakia. Due to Czechia's central location in Europe, recipes were exchanged by ethnic minorities, new settlers, in border towns, leading to a cuisine with amorphous borders and regional dishes spreading beyond Czech lands. Recipes also served as cultural capital, playing a role in the building of communities and of relationships between these communities."],["The body of Czech meals typically consists of at least two courses; the first course traditionally being soup, and the second course being the main dish. Third courses often consisted of a sweet dessert or compote (kompot) on more festive occasions."],["In Czech cuisine, thick soups are accompanied by stewed vegetables and meats in gravies and cream sauces. Czech cuisine has a reputation for having sweeter main courses than dishes in surrounding countries.  Meals are often accompanied by alcoholic drinks such as beer or wine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"czech-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Czech_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":10,"links":[["Buchteln","dish/buchteln"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Kolach (bread)","dish/kolach-bread"],["Kulajda","dish/kulajda"],["Potato pancake","dish/potato-pancake"],["Potato salad","dish/potato-salad"],["Sauerkraut","dish/sauerkraut"],["Steak tartare","dish/steak-tartare"],["Strudel","dish/strudel"],["Székelykáposzta","dish/szekelykaposzta"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Cook Like Czechs","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cooklikeczechs.com","note":"English-language blog of traditional Czech home recipes by a Czech cook.","url":"https://cooklikeczechs.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Curd","ingredient/curd"],["Fruit preserves","ingredient/fruit-preserves"],["Jam","ingredient/jam"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Peel","ingredient/peel"],["Poppy seed","ingredient/poppy-seed"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Rye flour","ingredient/rye-flour"],["Dill","ingredient/dill"],["Mushroom","ingredient/mushroom"],["Quail","ingredient/quail"],["Sour cream","ingredient/sour-cream"],["DL-lactic acid","ingredient/dl-lactic-acid"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Horse meat","ingredient/horse-meat"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Czech cooking","count":1,"links":[["Moravian cuisine","cuisine/moravian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/43/Knedlo_zelo_vep%C5%99o_%283%29.jpg/500px-Knedlo_zelo_vep%C5%99o_%283%29.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Pavel Ševela","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knedlo_zelo_vep%C5%99o_(3).jpg"}},"cuisine/haida-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/haida-foodways","n":"Haida foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Prince of Wales Island, Southeast Alaska","names":"Kaigani Haida (Xaadas)","intro":[["The Kaigani Haida live on southern Prince of Wales Island, around Hydaburg, with most Haida across the border in Haida Gwaii. Like their Tlingit neighbors, they eat from the forest and the sea: ",["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"],", halibut, shellfish, seaweed, ",["Herring","ingredient/herring"]," eggs and berries."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Alaska Native Heritage Center","url":"https://www.alaskanative.net/"},{"name":"University of Alaska Museum of the North, Alaska Native cultures guide","url":"https://uaf.edu/museum/education/docents/pdfs/NC2-3_Intro_2014.pdf"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"haida-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"ingredient/ice":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ice","n":"Ice","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Gazpacho","dish/gazpacho"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Gazpacho","dish/gazpacho"]]],["Turkish cuisine","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",[["Bici bici","dish/bici-bici"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Syrup","ingredient/syrup"],["Corn starch","ingredient/corn-starch"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Pandan","ingredient/pandan"],["Shaved ice","ingredient/shaved-ice"],["Avocado","ingredient/avocado"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Platostoma palustre","ingredient/platostoma-palustre"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tapioca pearl","ingredient/tapioca-pearl"],["Basil","ingredient/basil"],["Cucumis melo","ingredient/cucumis-melo"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/500px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Sharon Mollerus","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_(32752478892).jpg"}},"ingredient/spaghetti":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/spaghetti","n":"Spaghetti","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 6 dishes across 5 cuisines","intro":[["Spaghetti is a long, thin, solid, cylindrical pasta. It serves as a staple food of Italian cuisine and is made of milled wheat and water (sometimes enriched with vitamins and minerals), with the standard Italian version using durum-wheat semolina. The pasta is usually white because refined flour is used, but whole wheat flour may be added. Spaghettoni is a thicker form of spaghetti, while spaghettini is a thinner form. Capellini is a very thin spaghetti, while vermicelli is intermediate."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-spaghetti"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spaghetti"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":5,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Spaghetti alla Nerano","dish/spaghetti-alla-nerano"]]],["Yoshoku","cuisine/yoshoku",[["Naporitan","dish/naporitan"],["Toruko rice","dish/toruko-rice"]]],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"]]],["Italian-American cuisine","cuisine/italian-american-cuisine",[["Spaghetti and meatballs","dish/spaghetti-and-meatballs"]]],["Lazian or Roman","cuisine/roman-cuisine",[["Cacio e pepe","dish/cacio-e-pepe"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Pasta","ingredient/pasta"],["Pecorino romano","ingredient/pecorino-romano"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Pecorino Romano PDO","ingredient/pecorino-romano-pdo"],["Spaghetti alla chitarra","ingredient/spaghetti-alla-chitarra"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Guanciale","ingredient/guanciale"],["Pancetta","ingredient/pancetta"],["Pecorino","ingredient/pecorino"],["Agaricus bisporus","ingredient/agaricus-bisporus"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Ketchup","ingredient/ketchup"],["Sausage","ingredient/sausage"],["Tabasco sauce","ingredient/tabasco-sauce"],["Basil","ingredient/basil"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/9/92/Spaghettoni.jpg/500px-Spaghettoni.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Nnaluci","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spaghettoni.jpg"}},"dish/dak-galbi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/dak-galbi","n":"Dak-galbi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from South Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Dak-galbi, or spicy stir-fried chicken, is a popular South Korean dish made by stir-frying marinated diced chicken in a gochujang-based sauce with sweet potatoes, cabbage, Korean perilla, spring onion, tteok (rice cake), and other ingredients. Lettuce and perilla leaves may be served as ssam (wrap) vegetables. Many dak-galbi restaurants have round hot plates that are built into the tables."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-dak-galbi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dak-galbi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Korean Bapsang","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from South Korean cuisine","links":[["Chalbori-ppang","dish/chalbori-ppang"],["Chimaek","dish/chimaek"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Gilgeori toast","dish/gilgeori-toast"],["Hodu-gwaja","dish/hodu-gwaja"],["Hwangnam-ppang","dish/hwangnam-ppang"],["Jinju bibimbap","dish/jinju-bibimbap"],["Jjolmyeon","dish/jjolmyeon"],["Kkulppang","dish/kkulppang"],["Milmyeon","dish/milmyeon"],["Rabokki","dish/rabokki"],["So-tteok-so-tteok","dish/so-tteok-so-tteok"],["Tornado potato","dish/tornado-potato"],["Ttongppang","dish/ttongppang"],["Yangnyeom chicken","dish/yangnyeom-chicken"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/3/32/Korean_cuisine-Dakgalbi-01.jpg/500px-Korean_cuisine-Dakgalbi-01.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"by shizu k (shezzz) at Flickr","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean_cuisine-Dakgalbi-01.jpg"}},"dish/hog-maw":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/hog-maw","n":"Hog maw","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Chinese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Hog maw is the stomach of a pig prepared as food. More specifically, it is the exterior muscular wall of the stomach organ (with interior, lining mucosa removed) which contains no fat if cleaned properly. It can be found in American, soul food, Chinese, Pennsylvania Dutch, Mexican, German, Portuguese, Italian and  Vietnamese dishes. In addition, it can be prepared in various ways including stewed, fried, baked, and broiled."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-hog-maw"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hog_maw"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Woks of Life","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thewoksoflife.com","note":"Chinese and Chinese American recipes from the Leung family, running since 2013.","url":"https://thewoksoflife.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Chinese Cooking Demystified","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"youtube.com","note":"Technique-heavy YouTube channel on regional Chinese home and restaurant cooking.","url":"https://www.youtube.com/@ChineseCookingDemystified","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Red House Spice","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"redhousespice.com","note":"Classic Chinese recipes from Wei Guo, who grew up in Gansu, blogging since 2017.","url":"https://redhousespice.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Chinese cuisine","links":[["Almond biscuit","dish/almond-biscuit"],["Banana roll","dish/banana-roll"],["Baozi","dish/baozi"],["Bear paw","dish/bear-paw"],["Black sesame roll","dish/black-sesame-roll"],["Century egg","dish/century-egg"],["Cha siu bao","dish/cha-siu-bao"],["Chinese restaurant","dish/chinese-restaurant"],["Dandan noodles","dish/dandan-noodles"],["Dapanji","dish/dapanji"],["Dong'an chicken","dish/dong-an-chicken"],["Dongpo pork","dish/dongpo-pork"],["Donkey burger","dish/donkey-burger"],["Doujiang","dish/doujiang"],["Drunken shrimp","dish/drunken-shrimp"],["Edible bird's nest","dish/edible-bird-s-nest"],["Fuqi feipian","dish/fuqi-feipian"],["Gaifan","dish/gaifan"],["Goubuli","dish/goubuli"],["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"],["Hong dou tang","dish/hong-dou-tang"],["Hot dry noodles","dish/hot-dry-noodles"],["Hot pot","dish/hot-pot"],["Huangqiao sesame cake","dish/huangqiao-sesame-cake"],["Imitation shark fin soup","dish/imitation-shark-fin-soup"],["Jiaozi","dish/jiaozi"],["Jiuqu","dish/jiuqu"],["Kung Pao chicken","dish/kung-pao-chicken"],["Lanzhou beef noodles","dish/lanzhou-beef-noodles"],["Liangfen","dish/liangfen"],["Lo mai chi","dish/lo-mai-chi"],["Mao xue wang","dish/mao-xue-wang"],["Mapo tofu","dish/mapo-tofu"],["Mianwo","dish/mianwo"],["Miso soup","dish/miso-soup"],["Orange cuttlefish","dish/orange-cuttlefish"],["Osmanthus cake","dish/osmanthus-cake"],["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Red braised pork belly","dish/red-braised-pork-belly"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/b0/Hog_maw.jpg/500px-Hog_maw.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Quinn Dombrowski","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hog_maw.jpg"}},"ingredient/saffron":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/saffron","n":"Saffron","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 10 dishes across 11 cuisines","intro":[["Saffron is a spice derived from the sterile autumn-blooming flower of Crocus sativus, commonly known as the \"saffron crocus\". The vivid crimson stigma and styles (in industry jargon: 'threads') are collected and dried for use mainly as a food seasoning and colourant. Saffron crocus was slowly propagated throughout much of Eurasia and later brought to parts of North Africa, North America, even as far as Fiji in Oceania by its Indian migrant descendant population."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-saffron"},{"kind":"wiki","ur