.jpg"}},"ingredient/pigeon-pea":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pigeon-pea","n":"Pigeon pea","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["The pigeon pea (Cajanus cajan) is a perennial legume from the family Fabaceae native to the Eastern Hemisphere. The pigeon pea is widely cultivated in tropical and semitropical regions around the world, being commonly consumed in the Indian Subcontinent, Southeast Asia, Africa, Latin America and the Caribbean."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pigeon-pea"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pigeon_pea"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine",[["Kadyos, baboy, kag langka","dish/kadyos-baboy-kag-langka"],["Kadyos, manok, kag ubad","dish/kadyos-manok-kag-ubad"]]],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao",[["Kadyos, baboy, kag langka","dish/kadyos-baboy-kag-langka"],["Kadyos, manok, kag ubad","dish/kadyos-manok-kag-ubad"]]],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas",[["Kadyos, baboy, kag langka","dish/kadyos-baboy-kag-langka"],["Kadyos, manok, kag ubad","dish/kadyos-manok-kag-ubad"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Pigeon peas, dry","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":4927268,"top":[["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",3755066],["Malawi","cuisine/malawian-cuisine",440748],["Myanmar","cuisine/burmese-cuisine",300809],["Tanzania","cuisine/tanzanian-cuisine",192549],["Kenya","cuisine/kenyan-cuisine",133577],["Dominican Rep.",null,24194],["Nepal","cuisine/nepalese-cuisine",14801],["Uganda","cuisine/ugandan-cuisine",13513],["Benin","cuisine/benin-cuisine",12269],["Haiti","cuisine/haitian-cuisine",10936],["Peru","cuisine/peruvian-cuisine",8536],["Dem. Rep. Congo","cuisine/congolese-cuisine",6891],["Burundi","cuisine/burundian-cuisine",2616],["Colombia","cuisine/colombian-cuisine",2584],["Venezuela","cuisine/venezuelan-cuisine",2195]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Garcinia binucao","ingredient/garcinia-binucao"],["Jackfruit","ingredient/jackfruit"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Banana pith","ingredient/banana-pith"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/9/92/Cajanus_cajan_Blanco1.167-cropped.jpg/500px-Cajanus_cajan_Blanco1.167-cropped.jpg","w":500,"h":684,"by":"Francisco Manuel Blanco (O.S.A.)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cajanus_cajan_Blanco1.167-cropped.jpg"}},"dish/lekach":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/lekach","n":"Lekach","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Jewish cuisine","dot":"#a99fb5","intro":[["Lekach is a honey-sweetened cake made by Jews, especially for the Jewish holiday of Rosh Hashanah. Known in Hebrew as ugat dvash, it is mainly eaten in Israel by Israeli Jews and Jewish people all over the world who know it by its Yiddish name, lekach (לעקעך), phono-semantically matched in Hebrew as [ugat] lekakh ([עוּגַת] לֶקַח, lit. 'lesson cake') influenced by the Biblical association of teaching with honey. It is traditionally eaten at Rosh Hashanah in hopes of ensuring a sweet New Year. It is also customary to ask for and receive a honey cake on Erev Yom Kippur among Chabad Jews."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-lekach"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lekach"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Tori Avey: Jewish Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"toriavey.com","note":"Tori Avey's Jewish recipe index, often paired with notes on each dish's history.","url":"https://toriavey.com/jewish-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"The Nosher (My Jewish Learning)"